Message-ID: <199904011431.GAA27710@desire.rt.cs.boeing.com>

In Splus 5 for Unix (Sparc Solaris), [and S4 on the same platform, if
anyone cares] the standard unix interrupt [SIGINT] (control-c for me)
does not work, and in fact seems to break things.  If I attempt to
interrupt something that is printing to the screen, the interrupt does
not stop the output (as it did in Splus3), and after that Splus is
basically unusable because somehow "dput" has lost track of standard
output and /dev/tty.

My only recourse seems to be to stop and re-start S. 

Is there a work-around or fix?  Are others having the same problem, or
is this something specific to my local environment.  I have tried to
eliminate all things that might be specific to me, but I may have
missed something.

Appended below is a short transcript that shows the problem.  I have 
tried to make sure that I am not making any unusual settings.  After a 
control-C if I ask for output (like just typing "ls" to see the ls 
defintion), I get an error about dput and /dev/tty.   Interrupting a
function call seems to be OK---I only see the problem when
interrupting some output to the screen.

Thanks, --Mike

