The way we have commonly deal with non-detects is to use one-half of the
limit (i.e. one half of 9=4.5) this is somewhat better than using the limit
if you can assume that the non-detect values are randomly distributed below
the limit, which may or may not be a reasonable assumption, depending on
the assay. I think there are different approaches to dealing with
non-detects depending on the particular assay.

Tim

