>>> <eric.gibson at pharma.Novartis.com> 04/02 11:04 AM >>>

Gilliom and Helsel have 2 very nice papers in the Water Resources Research
journal
that discuss exactly this problem (sorry they are at home in boxes somewhere
otherwise
I'd supply the volume and year, but my memory isn't what it used to be).
Their article is referenced in many other articles on this same subject.

In environmental monitoring there are many regulatory issues involved.
Therefore one has to be careful in substituting arbitrary proxy values to
replace what was below the limit of detection or quantitation.
Simply using half the detection limits certainly seems reasonable,
but can induce bias in favor of the monitor or induce bias in favor of the
company being monitored, both of which are unacceptable, and could result in
litigation.

Gilliom and Helsel answer the question in their two-part paper as to what
apporoach is best.
The answer involves using a probability plot on those values above the detection
limit, and
then interpolating back into the nondetect region to get a proxy value.  But I
don't want to spoil the
ending completely, so I'll leave the reading to you.
They demonstrate via a huge simulation study that the approach of substituting
half the
detection limit loses badly (and using zero or the detection limit are even
worse)
in terms of MSE when estimating parameters like the mean and standard deviation
of a distribution.

This is a common problem in biostatistics and is encountered frequently in
bioassy samples.
In fact, it is nearly identical in that it also involves regulatory issues and
instead of groundwater samples
being assayed by an analytical chemist they are blood or urine samples assayed
at a medical lab.

I hope you find this helpful.

Best wishes,
