>>>>> "Tang" == Haicheng Tang <haicheng at umich.edu> writes:

    Tang> Dear Splus users,

    Tang> Generally a variable used in a function is internal and
    Tang> will not be saved in .Data once the function stops.  I
    Tang> wonder if anyone can tell me how to save some
    Tang> variable(s) in a function to .Data.  One way to see it
    Tang> is to cat it in the function, but sometimes saving it
    Tang> in .Data is preferred.

    Tang> Thanks in advance for any suggestion!

This is known as giving the function a side effect, and
particularly if you are writing sofware for others to use you are
recommended generally NOT to do it.  If you are writing functions
for yourself alone to use then you are still recommended against
doing it simply as a matter of safety.

If you really must do it (for instance because your supervisor,
possibly an ignorant and impatient old man only able to
understand macro languages insists on it) then you can use either

        assign("variable", value, where = 1)
or
        variable <<- value 

inside the function.  You are, however, solely responsible for
the consequences when someone complains that your function killed
their data... :-)

Bill Venables.

