From Melania_Pintilie at pmh.toronto.on.ca  Tue Apr  6 14:11:24 1999
From: Melania_Pintilie at pmh.toronto.on.ca (Pintilie, Melania)
Date: Tue, 06 Apr 1999 10:11:24 -0400
Subject: [S] imputation-overview
Message-ID: <99Apr6.101623edt.115675@gateway.healthlink.on.ca>

Hi everybody, Last week I have posted the following :

Suppose that there is an assay for which the value is not detectable when it
drops under 9. The median of the assay is 15 , the largest value is 80 the
75-th percentile is 30. Up to now in the literature all the values under 9
were considered exactly=9. This way of looking of the data overestimates the
mean and underestimates the standard deviation. 
My question is:
Is tere a way to deal with this problem?
Can these values be imputed ?

It have received a great deal of response and I will try to summarize it
here. The order does not reflect any preference.
Thank you all for their  interest. It is certainly great to be part of
S-news. 
1) to treat the data as left censored data.
2)  to use maximum likelihood 
3) to replace the missing with the half point ;e.g. here with 4.5.  
4) to make assumptions on how the data is distributed based on the data
which exists (9-80) and impute the missing values based on that
distribution.
REF
 a)AUTHOR   = Helsel, Dennis R.
AUTHOR   = Gilliom, Robert J.
TITLE  = Estimation of distributional parameters for censored trace level
         water quality data 2. Verification and applications
JOURNAL  = Water Resources Research
VOLUME   = 22
PAGES  = 147-155
YEAR   = 1986
 b) Estimation of distributional parameters for censored trace level water
quality data" Water Resources Research v.22, 1986, Parts "1. Estimation
techniques",  pp.135-146 and "2. Verification and applications", pp.
147-155?
5) to use extrreme value theory
6) to use trimmed mean for locator estimation
 
Other ref: 
 Statistical Methods in Water Resources
       D.R. Helsel and R.M. Hirsch
      Elsevier, 1992

"Truncated and censored samples from normal populations" by Helmut
Schneider and edited by Marcel Dekker (1986).
ISBN 0-8247-7591-0.

 Chung, C.F., 1988. Statistical analysis of truncated data in geosciences,
in Sci. de la Terr, Ser.
Inf., Nancy, Vol. 27, p. 157-180.

 Chung, C.F., 1985. Statistical treatment of geochemical data with
observations below the
detection limit; in Current Research, Part B, Geological Survey of Canada,
Paper 85-1B, p. 141-
150. - with Fortran program

 Sanford, R.F, Pierson, C.T., and Crovelli, R.A., 1993: An Objective
Replacement Method for
Censored Geochemical Data, Mathematical Geology, Volume 25, Number 1, pp.
59-80.
Helsel and Cohn, 1988. Estimation of descriptive statistics for multiply 
censored water quality data. Water Resources Research, v22 n12, pp 
1997-2004.

El-Shaarawi, 1989. Inferences about the mean form censored water quality 
data. Water Resources Research v25 n2 pp 685-690.

El-Shaarawi and Esterby, 1992. Replacement of censored observations by a 
constant: and evaluation.  Water Research v26 n6 pp 835-844.

Haas and Scheff, 1990. Estimation of averages in truncated samples. 
Environmental Science and Technology v24 n6 pp 912-919.

For a less technical review:

Helsel, 1990. Less than obvious: statistical treatment of data below the 
detection limit. Environmental Science and Technology v24 n12 pp 1767-1774.

Also, in the S-Plus world, Steve Millard has developed functions that apply 
these and other methods in his Environmetal Stats for S-Plus module.  As he 
is a frequent contributor to this group, I'm sure he will be commenting on 
this issue soon.

Using 9 or 4.5 still would undersetimate the underlying variance, which
will bias the significance of subsequent statistical analysis. To retain
the underlying variance, multiple imputation may work for you. There's a
software for Splus to do this. Visit:
http://www.stat.psu.edu/~jls/

He also has a textbook on this topic.

Check
Greene's _Econometric Analysis_ for approaches, or there are many other
references (Maddala 1983 (1997) is pretty good, too); censored
distributions are very similar.  Tobit, Heckman selection models, and ML
approaches similar to the Heckman seem relevant, depending on the modeling
method you need to use.

Porter S, Ward RC, Bell HF: The detection limit. Environ. Sci. Technol
22(8):856-861 (1988)
The usual method is known as Tobit regression or censored Normal
regression.  In using it, you need to be sure that the non-censored values
come from an approximately Normal distribution.  If necessary you may have
to
transform the data (Take logs etc.)
It is implemented in some good statistical packages - such as Stata.

Chatterjee S and Hadi AS 1988 Sensitivity analysis in linear regression. New
York: John Wiley & Sons

Check:
Tweedie: on Funnel plots

