From wvenable at arcola.stats.adelaide.edu.au  Thu Apr  8 22:59:50 1999
From: wvenable at arcola.stats.adelaide.edu.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Fri, 09 Apr 1999 08:29:50 +0930
Subject: Re: [S][second try] assignment problem inside a function (fwd)
In-Reply-To: <370C9BE6.5FFA8CAA@genedata.com>
References: <Pine.SOL.3.95.990408105324.18143E-100000@alp> <370C9BE6.5FFA8CAA@genedata.com>
Message-ID: <14093.13542.689877.231402@arcola.stats.adelaide.edu.au>

>>>>> "Andreas" == Andreas Krause <Andreas.Krause at genedata.com> writes:

    Andreas> This is a problem that shows up in several places.
    Andreas> The problem is (as it looks like) in sum, an
    Andreas> internal routine.  Setting na.rm=T causes all NA
    Andreas> elements to be discarded before summing up.  If all
    Andreas> elements are missing, the remainder is nothing, an
    Andreas> element of length 0 like logical(0), numeric(0), or
    Andreas> NULL.  Then however, sum(nothing) gives 0.

    Andreas> I think I recall a discussion with Bill Dunlap years
    Andreas> ago, where he had reasons for this behavior, I just
    Andreas> can't recall what the point was (sorry if I'm wrong,
    Andreas> Bill).

This is in line with a well established mathematical convention,
of course.  A sum with no terms is defined to be zero and a
product with no terms is defined to be one.  Essentially sum()
and prod() are defined relative to these starting points and what
you see is the effect on them, so if there are no terms there is
no change.  It simplifies things a great deal.

You will also note that prod(NULL) is 1.  It's a convention that
pays off in the end.

Bill Venables.

