From callahan at envstat.com  Fri Apr  9 13:46:04 1999
From: callahan at envstat.com (Ed Callahan)
Date: Fri, 09 Apr 1999 08:46:04 -0500
Subject: [S] Summary: modified matrix mult
Message-ID: <000101be828f$50623460$a40fa3d1@rconnect.com>

I had asked if there were a straight forward way to "multiply" two matrices
in a non-standard way. Instead of the standard b <- a%*%t(a) resulting in
b[i,j] = sum(a[i,]*a[j,]) I wanted b[i,j] = sum(pmin(a[i,],a[j,])).

My only solution was to use loops like:

  b <- matrix(NA, nrow(a), nrow(a))
  for(i in 1:nrow(a)){
        for(j in 1:nrow(a)){
                b[i,j] <- sum(pmin(a[i,], a[j,]))
        }
  }

which was slow and didn't seem very "S-like". I was offered alternative
solutions from Charles Berry, Nick Ellis, Erin Hodges and Peter Perkins.
Each involved expanding the matrix into larger versions. For instance:

  n1 <- nrow(a)
  x3 <- pmin(a[rep(1:n1,each=n1),],a[rep(1:n1,n1),])
  x4 <- matrix(apply(x3,1,sum),nrow=n1,ncol=n1)
  x4

and

  matrix( pmin(x[rep(1:nrow(x),ncol(y)),],
          t(y[,rep(1:ncol(y),rep(nrow(x),ncol(y)))]))%*%
          rep(1,ncol(x)), ncol(y))

Since I was working with moderately large matrices these solutions were
still a little slow. I ended up coding the problem in a C routine that could
be called with .C() and the speed increase was amazing.

Thanks for all the tips.

Ed

