From Gerald.Jean at spgdag.ca  Fri Apr  9 19:22:51 1999
From: Gerald.Jean at spgdag.ca (=?utf-8?q?G=C3=A9rald_Jean?=)
Date: Fri, 09 Apr 1999 15:22:51 -0400
Subject: [S] Summary: Over-dispersion in Poisson models
Message-ID: <8525674E.0068D4FB.00@mail.spgdag.com>

About a week ago I posted the following problem:

>Dear Splus users,

>I have a problem somewhat related to Roy Robertson's regarding
over-dispersed Poisson models.

>I am trying to develop, in a first step, a Poisson model for a data set
with a very large number of cells (140,000) and roughly 20 >categorical
variables with several levels each.  From the start I suspected
over-dispersion and tried to estimate it fitting the full >model to the
data using the function glm with family=quasi(link='log',var='mu').  But
glm runs for more than an hour to finally tell >me that convergence was not
attained in 10 iterations, it still returns parameter estimates and also an
estimate of dispersion of >roughly 7.8...........  Now if I run glm for the
NULL model with the other arguments remaining the same I get an estimate of
>2.4....... for the dispersion.  I would like to use the dispersion to help
me select the most significant drivers (probably using the >function add1)
to get the modeling started; of course the significance level of the
variables is dependent on the value of the >dispersion parameter.

>I know that I could increse the default number of iterations in glm, but I
am reluctant to do it since it takes so much time to run >the way it is
now.

>I am running Splus 4.5 under Windows NT on a pretty powerfull machine
(Pentium II, 350 Mz, 128 M of RAM).

>Any suggestions on how to tackle the problem?

>Thanks in advance,

I got a few replies, many thanks to those who gave it a try.  Only one
suggestion finaly led to a solution thanks to Charles Berry.

Charles suggested an S function to do Iterative Proportional Fitting.
Before getting his function to run I had realized that I had a continuous
variable in my potential drivers which had no business there, hence I reran
glm with family=quasi....... and got convergence this time.  I got an
estimate of dispersion of 2.160158 and it took roughly 1h.15min. for glm to
converge.  Charles function produced fitted values from which I got an
estimate of dispersion of 2.16344, Charles function took roughly 2.5 min.
to run; not bad!

Here is the function:

rake.tab <- function (x,marg,indices,tol=1e-06, iter = 20 )
{
#  Author  : Charles C. Berry
#  Date    : 1999
#  Purpose : Charles' implementation of Iterative Proportional Fitting
(IPF)
#  Parameters :

### x       : is a starting value, try a vector of ones of the same length
as
###           the number of rows in the data matrix(frame).  If the rows of

###           your.matrix[,c(col numbers of categorical vars.)] are all
unique,
###           then x <- rep(1,nrow(your.matrix)) will work fine.  If rows
are not
###           unique they must be collapsed first.
### marg    : is a list of vectors of marginal (columns) counts -
###           try marg[[1]] <- sapply(split(your.data,indices[[1]]),sum),
etc.
### indices : is a list of indexes to your categorical variables -
###           same length as the number of rows in the data. e.g.
###           indices <- list(
###           name of 1st categ. var = your.matrix[,col index of 1st categ
var],
###           name of 2nd categ. var = your.matrix[,col index of 2nd categ
var],
###           .
###           .
###           .
###           name of nth categ. var = your.matrix[,col index of nth categ
var])

apply.sum <- function(x,marg )
{
#  Author  : Charles C. Berry
#  Date    : 1999

### rake tab uses this function
  dimX     <- dim(x)
  first    <- seq(along=dimX)[-marg]
  new.dim  <- c(prod(dimX[first]),prod(dimX[marg]))
  perm     <- c(first,marg)
  res      <- array(1,c(1,new.dim[1]))%*%array(aperm(x,perm),new.dim)
  dim(res) <- dimX[marg]
res
}

  len.x   <- length(x)
  updates <- lapply(marg,function(x) rep(1,length(x)))
  max.adj <- Inf
  while( (iter <- iter -1)>0 && max.adj > tol)
    {

      max.adj <- 0
      for (i in seq(along=indices))
        {
          if (length(indices[[i]])==len.x)
            comp.marg <- sapply(split(x,indices[[i]]),sum)
          else
            comp.marg <- apply.sum(x,indices[[i]])
          adj             <- marg[[i]]/comp.marg
          adj[is.na(adj)] <- 0
          max.adj         <- max(c(max.adj,abs(log(adj))),na.rm=T)
          if (length(indices[[i]])!=len.x)
            x <- sweep(x,indices[[i]],adj,"*")
          else
            x <- x*adj[indices[[i]]]
          updates[[i]] <- updates[[i]]*adj
        }
    }
  list(iter=iter,fit=x,updates=updates,max.adj=max.adj)
}

