From zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu  Wed Apr 14 11:29:21 1999
From: zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu (Alan Zaslavsky)
Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1999 07:29:21 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] computing tied ranks
Message-ID: <199904141129.HAA29885@nightingale.hcp.med.harvard.edu>

Michael Wolf <mwolf at est-econ.uc3m.es> asks:
> I am not sure if the following can be considered a bug. It comes up when
> computing ranks of differences. Even though some differences are tied,
> the "naive" way of computing their ranks does not pick this up.
> > x
> [1] 2.4 1.3 1.5 0.9
> > y
> [1] 2.1 0.9 2.0 0.6
> > x - y
> [1]  0.3  0.4 -0.5  0.3
> > rank(x - y)
> [1] 2 4 1 3

Anything that depends on exact equality tests of floating point (noninteger)
numbers cannot be relied upon.  This is inherent in the representation of
numbers in floating point, not a bug.  In your example, of the four
numbers in x (2.4 1.3 1.5 0.9) and four in y (2.1 0.9 2.0 0.6), only two
(1.5 and 2.0) have exact floating point representations (integers+binary
fractions).  Consequently, the internal representations are approximate
and arithmetic on them produces results with small and not readily
predictable (except by doing the calculation!) errors in the least
significant bits.  The rank() function only finds a tie when there is
exact equality.

If you really are interested in finding ties, you will have to explicitly
introduce a tolerance by rounding or some other method.  Because double
precision floating point numbers have approximately 14 decimal digit
accuracy, and your values are all less than 10, you could have used
rank(round(x-y,12)) and be pretty safe.

> rank(x-y)
[1] 2 4 1 3
> rank(round(x-y,12))
[1] 2.5 4.0 1.0 2.5
