From asinclair at edfman.co.uk  Wed Apr 14 13:24:00 1999
From: asinclair at edfman.co.uk (sinclair, andrew (MIP London))
Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1999 14:24:00 +0100
Subject: [S] computing tied ranks
Message-ID: <B0000085083@mimesweeper.edfman.co.uk>

Floating point equality is a subject which has appeared
a few times on s-news,  and I have been wondering if
there is a better solution.

Surely one could modify the "==" operator so that it behaves
"more intelligently" for floating point numbers.  For a naive
example one could have:

equals.fp _ function(x1,x2){
     if( is.null( options("eqtol")[[1]] ) )  return(x1==x2)
     else return(abs(x1-x2)<options("eqtol")[[1]] )
     }

> (x-y)[1] == (x-y)[4]
[1] F
> equals.fp((x-y)[1], (x-y)[4])  ## behaves as ==
[1] F
> options("eqtol"=1e-10)
> equals.fp((x-y)[1], (x-y)[4])  ## after setting "eqtol" gives desired 
rounding
[1] T

The details of how to get this functionality to be part
of "==" rather than a separate function are beyond
my ken, and may not even be possible.

Of course equals.fp() is not scalable - if your
numbers are both of the order of 1e-10 then it may
think they are equal even if they differ by a factor
of 2. The opposite problem may occur if they are of
order 1e10.  In these cases one would have to either
avoid this work around, or perhaps modify "eqtol" .

Unfortunately this would not change the output from
rank() unless one could do a similar thing to the
internal function duplicated() ... !!

Andrew
