I was unaware of all.equal.  It does what I
meant, although it would not be very convenient
to use regularly since it is roughly equivalent
to  all(x==y)  ie does not return a vector answer
when used with two vector arguments.

I guess I was really saying that _personally_ when I
test two floating points for equality I more often
mean in the sense of all.equal() than in the exact sense
of == , and I believe this to be true of others also (hence
this being a commonly asked question).

So for me (and others?) it would have been better if the
functionality was reversed in the case of floating point
equality tests (because == is definitely more readable).

Andrew

