From devon.mccormick at bankerstrust.com  Wed Apr 14 16:32:32 1999
From: devon.mccormick at bankerstrust.com
Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1999 12:32:32 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] Project Management in S+
Message-ID: <85256753.005ADBAD.00@NYSMTP4000.svc.btco.com>

On 04/13/99, feldesmanm at pdx.edu asks

> What other ways do people have of managing the plenitude of objects that
> get created during an S-Plus (4.5R2, Win98) session?

> How else do you keep files associated with one project separate from
files
> associated with another project?

A method I use to separate and track multiple projects in S+
is to keep them in separate sub-directories under some generically
named top-level directory, e.g. "Global".  Also at this top level
is a pointer file "cdfile.dat".  This file has a single
line pointing to the current project I'm working on, e.g.
"c:\Global\Proj1".  Whenever I switch projects, I edit this file
to specify which one I'm working on.

My icons, to either start S-Plus or run an S-Plus batch job,
all run a short script to read this file and set the S_PROJ
parameter accordingly.  This scheme lets me keep projects
segregated in their own directories but makes it easy to
switch between them with no more than 1 or 2 icons on my desktop.

To summarize with an example, we might have 4 projects (e.g. Domestic,
Internat, Euro, and Y2K) in a directory structure like this:

    Global
    |   cdfile.dat
    +---Domestic
    |   +---_DATA
    |   \---_PREFS
    +---Internat
    |   +---_DATA
    |   \---_PREFS
    +---Euro
    |   +---_DATA
    |   \---_PREFS
    \---Y2K
        +---_DATA
        \---_PREFS

The contents of cdfile.dat might be "C:\Global\Domestic" when
I'm working on the project "Domestic".

The (DOS batch) command script that my S-Plus icon invokes
contains the following:

    @echo off
    REM SPlus.bat: Start S-Plus (interactive session) using project
    REM  specified in file c:\Global\cdfile.dat.

    call setproj.bat c:\Global\cdfile.dat
    cd %PROJ%
    D:\Splus45\cmd\SPLUS.EXE S_PROJ=%PROJ% S_CMDFILE=+Hist.txt

The command script "setproj.bat" referred to above merely sets the
environment variable PROJ.  I use this same mechanism for code in
other languages.  Even though this example is PC-centric, the general
method should work fine in *nix environments.  When using this method,
it's a good idea to have any batch jobs output where they're running
from to ensure we're using the code we think we're using.

If anyone is interested in the setproj.bat script (and the perl
script it runs), send me e-mail at devon.mccormick at bankerstrust.com.

