From Terry.Elrod at UAlberta.ca  Wed Apr 14 17:18:10 1999
From: Terry.Elrod at UAlberta.ca (Terry Elrod)
Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1999 11:18:10 -0600
Subject: RE: [S] Project Management in S+
In-Reply-To: <4.1.19990413190907.0092e5f0@pop4.ibm.net>
References: <4.1.19990413190907.0092e5f0@pop4.ibm.net>
Message-ID: <000001be869a$c5118d60$5ce68081@hh3se>

I use different subdirectories to separate
projects.

Three other simple practices help a lot to manage
the plenitude of objects associated with a given
project:

(1) I use a period as the starting character of
any object that I do not expect to have permanent
value. If any of these come to have lasting value,
I rename them with long, descriptive names. I
regularly clear out these temporary names with a
function remove.temp:

"remove.temp"<-
function()
{
        .objects <- objects(patt = ".*")
        exceptions <- c(".First")
        remove(setdiff(.objects,
                exceptions))
}

The names for temporary objects can be only 2 or 3
characters because they need not be descriptive,
which spares the fingers some.

(2) I add a numeric extension to a name to
indicate subsequent generations, e.g. db, db2,
db3, ...

(3) I use the object browser and sort on the date
field, which displays the most recent objects of
any category at the bottom.

Terry Elrod

