That will automatically happen the way S-mode is used.  There's
something desirably simple about starting Splus in the directory where
your files are.  Shortcuts are a long way round.  If I were forced to
use Windows, I'd have the shortcut in the same directory as my files
and start S from within Explorer.  I'd still have to set up each
shortcut by hand.  Maybe ESS for Windows Emacs would avoid that.

If anyone else is like me, they will also have a dozen or two text
files associated with each project.  One file is a list of the useful
commands I used for that project and which can be rerun line by line
in the *S* buffer if desired using a couple of key-strokes.  It also
contains comments which describe the method in my madness: very useful
when revisiting.  

I also invariably have postscript files of plots with each project.
It's common for researchers to desire small changes to be made to
labelling of plots.  Very often I can do that by editing the
postscript files (in Emacs) without any need to start Splus again.
It's very easy to find my way through a directory using dired (in
another Emacs buffer) and view the plot with pageview.  It is a very
efficient way to work.

Now, if I could just get some time to get ESS going, I would have work
done before it gets to me. :-)

best

Patrick

