From wvenable at arcola.stats.adelaide.edu.au  Thu Apr 15 03:07:47 1999
From: wvenable at arcola.stats.adelaide.edu.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Thu, 15 Apr 1999 12:37:47 +0930
Subject: Re: [S] computing tied ranks
In-Reply-To: <B0000085083@mimesweeper.edfman.co.uk>
References: <B0000085083@mimesweeper.edfman.co.uk>
Message-ID: <14101.22531.154141.901310@arcola.stats.adelaide.edu.au>

>>>>> "Andrew" == sinclair, andrew (MIP London) <asinclair at edfman.co.uk> writes:

    Andrew> Floating point equality is a subject which has
    Andrew> appeared a few times on s-news, and I have been
    Andrew> wondering if there is a better solution.

    Andrew> Surely one could modify the "==" operator so that it
    Andrew> behaves "more intelligently" for floating point
    Andrew> numbers.  For a naive example one could have:

...

    Andrew> The details of how to get this functionality to be
    Andrew> part of "==" rather than a separate function are
    Andrew> beyond my ken, and may not even be possible.

...

Explicitly modifying the == operator would be a step in the wrong
direction as it could disturb the behaviour of a lot of existing
code.  There is a better solution, (at least in principle) using
object orientation, for which this provides quite a nice example.

The idea is that you create a class of objects called, say,
"fuzzy.point".  For most purposes these behave entirely like the
numeric objects they are, but you write a short *group generic
function*, (which in S3 must be called Ops.fuzzy.point), that
provides the special actions needed when such objects are used
with logical or arithmetic operators.  In brief these are that

. == and != are checked with a tolerance, like a vector result
  version of all.equal, and 

. when fuzzy.point objects are used with arithmetic operators, the
  result is a fuzzy.point object (except for %/%, I suppose).

    Andrew> Unfortunately this would not change the output from
    Andrew> rank() unless one could do a similar thing to the
    Andrew> internal function duplicated() ... !!

The function duplicated() looks to be a generic function, too,
since it has an .Internal reference, so it should be possible to
write a method function, duplicated.fuzzy.point, to provide the
necessary object specific behaviour.  There would probably be
other generic functions that could usefully be given fuzzy.point
methods as well - in fact group generic functions Math.fuzzy.point
and Summary.fuzzy.point are obvious cases.  

The beauty of this approach is that you get everything you want
without disturbing existing code in any way.  The cost is probably
some small loss of efficiency, and the fact that you have to
construct and operate with fuzzy.point objects, but this is not at
all hard or bothersome.  It seems to me that operator overloading
is not used nearly as much as it might usefully be in S.

Bill Venables.

