On Wed, 14 Apr 1999, [iso-8859-1] Gérald Jean wrote:

> I have created a model matrix from several factors in a data.frame.
> 
> Suppose y is a continuous variable, f1, f2 and f3 are factors with several
> levels.
> Then I created a model.matrix the following way.
> 
> >my.model.matrix <- model.matrix (y ~ f1 + f2 + f3, data=my.data.frame)
> 
> I firsts setted the contrast option to what I wanted and this works fine.
> Now I would like to "cbind" my continous variable y and a weight variable,
> say w,
> to this model matrix and convert it to a data.frame.  Everything I tried
> failed.
> 
> >new.frame <- as.data.frame (my.model.matrix,y,w)
> >new.frame <- as.data.frame (cbind(my.model.matrix,y,w))
> >new.frame <- data.frame (my.model.matrix,y,w)
> >new.frame <- data.frame (cbind(my.model.matrix,y,w))
> 
> all these return me a data.frame with only 3 components, namely:
> my.model.matrix, y and w.
> 
> I would like to have as many components as in my.model.matrix + 2 (for y
> and w),
> I also wish I could preserve the column names of the model.matrix.

In so far as I understand this, you don't want the special behaviour of
the model.matrix class. So coerce my.model.matrix first.

> my.model.matrix <- model.matrix (Days ~ ., data=quine)
> class(my.model.matrix)
[1] "model.matrix" "matrix"      
> dimnames(my.model.matrix)[[2]]
[1] "(Intercept)" "Eth"         "Sex"         "Age1"        "Age2"       
[6] "Age3"        "Lrn"        

> data.frame(Days=quine$Days, unclass(my.model.matrix), check.names=F)
   Days (Intercept) Eth Sex Age1 Age2 Age3 Lrn 
 1    2           1  -1   1   -1   -1   -1   1
 2   11           1  -1   1   -1   -1   -1   1
 3   14           1  -1   1   -1   -1   -1   1

You need check.names=F as "(Intercept)" is not a legal column name
in a data frame otherwise.

