On 04/13/99, feldesmanm at pdx.edu asks

> What other ways do people have of managing the plenitude of objects that
> get created during an S-Plus (4.5R2, Win98) session?

> How else do you keep files associated with one project separate from
files
> associated with another project?

[I was away from the list when this was posted, but was alerted by other 
subscribers (Jane Elith and Tim Smith)].

I have a system for hierarchical project management in Splus, together with 
some other utilities, which has been distributed to 50-odd users over the past 
few years. You can change projects within an Splus session, and can move stuff 
from one project to another, etc. The software works with Splus 4.5 for 
windows, and there is also a partial version for 3.4 Windows/3.3 Unix (but I no 
longer update the latter). Please email me at

m.v.bravington at cefas.co.uk

if you'd like a copy. The latest version isn't (yet) available on a website-- 
Brian Ripley has kindly hosted one version of this software, but I confess to 
not having kept that version properly up to date.

Jane's unsolicited testimony follows!

I use (with windows v3.3 and 4.5) a set of utilities written by Mark Bravington 
(M.V.BRAVINGTON at cefas.co.uk) and first mentioned in this group a couple of 
years (or more??) ago.  They allow the creation of directories from within 
splus (with a command prompt that changes as you change directories). 
Directories can have a hierachical structure, and data from a parent directory 
can be read from within the child directory (very handy..  I bury my precious 
bits at the base of the hierachy). You can move and copy data between 
directories, move between  directories easily within a session, view the 
directory structure, find objects etc etc (ie other utility as well).  Here's 
an example of some of my directory structure:

> > cdtree()
ROOT
mvbutils
debug
anca
dat
now
        gam
        jen
glm
and
cass
..

> > cd(preds/new)
preds/new> move( test3, 0/utah, . ,copy=T) ..#this copies an object to the 
current directory
They're very easy to work with (I'm have no DOS or programming background), are 
well documented, and Mark has been exceptionally helpful with updates, advice 
etc. In case this will overburden him with requests I'll send the message to 
the group via him.

