From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Thu, 15 Apr 1999 21:58:51 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] Hosmer-Lemeshow Test of Goodness of Fit
Message-ID: <011f01be87ac$ae0f1020$43d5bfa8@fharrell.biostat.virginia.edu>

The Hosmer-Lemeshow test has limited power and depends on arbitrary
cut points on predicted probabilities.  A better (one degree of freedom) omnibus
test can be found in 

@ARTICLE{hos97com,
  author = {Hosmer, D. W. and Hosmer, T. and {le Cessie}, S. and Lemeshow, S.},
  year = 1997,
  title = {A comparison of goodness--of--fit tests for the logistic regression
          model},
  journal = Statistics in Medicine,
  volume = 16,
  pages = {965-980},
  annote = {goodness-of-fit for binary logistic model;difficulty with
           Hosmer-Lemeshow statistic being dependent on how groups are
           defined;sum of squares test;cumulative sum test;invalidity of naive
           test based on deviance;goodness-of-link function;simulation setup}
}

The new test is implemented in the resid function that is used with
the lrm function in the Design library.  This test is based on comparing
the sum of squared errors to the expected value of sum of squared errors
(sum of (Y - Phat)^2) if the model holds.

