From rossini at biostat.washington.edu  Fri Apr 16 14:25:42 1999
From: rossini at biostat.washington.edu (A.J. Rossini)
Date: Fri, 16 Apr 1999 07:25:42 -0700
Subject: Re: [S] Project management in Splus
Message-ID: <877lrcod5l.fsf@alpha.cfas.washington.edu>

Mark's project management mode is nice, but not exactly what I
like. Someone mentioned ESS (Emacs Speaks Statistics -- a general user
interface to Splus, R, SAS, XlispStat, to name a few), and while it
covers some of the features of project management, it doesn't do all
of them.  That is, it can be simply (via Emacs and GNU programming
tools) to incorporate:
        documentation 
        simple and easy reproducibility
        revision/version control (useful for backtracking to the point
                you were at a few hours, days, years ago, plus for
                documenting why/when you changed what you did) 
                (using CVS, this buys you multiple user access, which 
                 is useful when multiple statisticians are working on 
                 the same project, either as equals, or senior/junior 
                 supervision)

An extention to ESS that is under rapid development is integration
with a literate programming system, for what I'll term "Literate Data
Analysis".

Where as in S-PLUS, the real version of the data is stored in
cryptically coded files in you .Data/_Data directory, and in ESS, the
real version is stored in text files in a directory (common or local,
depending on your personal opinion), I'm currently working on storage
within a set of Noweb file/files, which use LaTeX or HTML for
documentation, and do appropriate things with "code chunks", which can
be put together.  For those of you familiar with LaTeX (or HTML),
everything that you can do with latex can be done in this situation.

The conservative estimate for release is the end of summer, it'll be
merged into the alpha versions of ESS in about 3 weeks, for trial
evaluation.

But, I'll say that Mark's package is more than adequate for most
needs, if you don't need or want to have a full programming IDE for
analysis.

best,
-tony

