>>>>> "Gregory" == Gregory R Warnes <warnes at biostat.washington.edu> writes:

    Gregory> R (the Splus clone) has a complete set of
    Gregory> distribution and random number generation functions.

And so it has.  

This is a mild suggestion only, but could we not refer to R as
"the Splus clone" any longer, please?  It gives the impression
that R is some kind of poor person's Splus, which is a cheap shot
at what is rapidly emerging as a mature and sophisticated
software system.

In reality they are not "clones" of each other, but two distinct
systems with a strikingly similar command line interface but with
very different internals, as anyone who uses both will be
(probably painfully) aware.  They have quite different strengths
and weaknesses which after a while you come to appreciate.
Programming is already markedly different between the two in many
fundamental respects, and when S-version 4 fully takes over as
the S-PLUS engine this difference will only increase.  I can see
the two systems diverging in other very useful and complementary
ways in the future.  Already I seem to need both, which would
never be the case if they truly were clones.

Bill Venables.
