From Gerald.Jean at spgdag.ca  Mon Apr 19 14:41:56 1999
From: Gerald.Jean at spgdag.ca (=?utf-8?q?G=C3=A9rald_Jean?=)
Date: Mon, 19 Apr 1999 10:41:56 -0400
Subject: [S] Summary: Diagonal matrix multiplication
Message-ID: <85256758.004E6DCF.00@mail.spgdag.com>

Thanks evryone for such a response (direct mailers, and statisticians who
develop their
models (I was one of them not too long ago), would be thrilled by such a
response rate!!!)

The original posting follows, along with the suggested solutions.

>Hello everyone,
>
>does anyone knows how to multiply a diagonal matrix, A say, with an
>arbitrary compatible matrix, B say, without expanding the diagonal matrix
A
>(using "diag", or anything else) or writing code that uses for loops?
>

First it seems that I wasn't clear enoug that I couldn't use diag(a) due to
the size of a and for the same reason for loops would be vey inefficient.

Karl Broman, Andrzej Tadeusz Galecki, Bill Dunlap, Albyn Jones, Alan
Zaslavsky, Rod Tjoelker and Claude Nadeau (my apologies if I am forgetting
anyone) basically all suggested the same solution.

If the vector "a" holds the diagonal elements of the diaganal matrix "A"
and "B" is the other matrix, assuming they are compatible.

A %*% B = diag(a) %*% B = a * b = b * a

which gives the solution of premultiplying B by A.

B %*% A = B %*% diag(a) = t(t(B) * a) = t(a * t(B))

which gives the solution for postmultiplying B by A.

Thanks to every one again,

