Subject: [S] singularities in using nlminb for a mixture of normals; and deriv3 of Venables and Ripley vs. deriv of Splus
Message-ID: <2F7D861DCB20D211AD6C00A0C99948A78966A3@CLTTMP22>

Dear Readers,

I will be glad to summarize and post responses & responder's names to the
following problem:

I'm attempting to fit a mixture of normals based on Venables and Ripley (2nd
Ed).
I began with their formulation on pages 288-289, providing method of moments
estimates
as the starting points for gradient descent (from Titterington et al.,
1985).  However, the
following problem occurred.  Using the notation of Venables and Ripley, I
fit the following (with lmix2 and tr.ms
as defined in Venables and Ripley):

        wait.mix2<-ms(~lmix2(x,p,u1,s1,u2,s2),
                start=p0, data=dataframe, trace=tr.ms)
        
...and the computations went as follows: 

# -279.125 : 0.913174  0.000643     0.006916   0.000643     0.016758 
# -356.977 : 5.65355   0.000540093  0.0221012  0.000674583  0.130697 
 Warning messages:
    Warning in log(.expr12): NAs generated
  Error in ms( ~ lmix2(x, p, u1, s1, u2, s2), s..: 81 NAs found in value
  Dumped

NA's occurred because the value of "p" (see 2nd row of iteration results) is
outside
the range 0-1 (it is 5.65...), so the value "1-p" in the likelihood equation
(Venables and Ripley, p.288) is negative, 
so the loglikelihood involves taking the log of a negative number which is
undefined, producing NAs.

So, I switched to using nlminb so I could place inequality constraints on
the optimization ("box constraints").
In particular, I'm using 
        0.001<=p<=0.999
        s1 and s2 in a large positive range (lower bound of the range is
s/50, where s is the sample standard
                deviation, to avoid s1 or s2 going to zero here, which can
cause trouble)
        u1 and u2 within 3 standard deviations of the sample mean.  

However, when I run nlminb using the following call (my approach here is
based on Venables and Ripley, page 292):

wait.mix2<-nlminb(p0,mix.obj,
        lower=lowerbound,
        upper=upperbound, x=dataframe[,1])

...I get the following warning:

Warning messages:
  Warning in nlminb.0(temp, p, liv, lv, objective, bounds, scale): 
        singularity encountered

When I examine wait.mix2 following this result, it shows that the values of
the 5 mixture of normal
parameters are exactly what were passed to nlminb.   

My questions are:
(1) What sort of singularity is being produced?
(2) How do I fix my formulation so as to avoid this problem?

and a somewhat related question
(3) How does Venables and Ripley's (p.288) "deriv3" in their MASS library 
differ from "deriv" as included in S-Plus?

I would be grateful for any help on this one.  

Best Regards
Matt Kurbat, Ph.D.

BELOW IS THE S-PLUS CODE USED:
path.function<-"H:\\My Documents\\SPLUS_MON\\"
path.library<-"C:\\Program Files\\splus45\\library\\"
funcfile<-"mon_lib.ssc"
funcfile<-paste(path.function,funcfile,sep="")

datafile<-"ddata2.xls"
datafile<-paste(path.function,datafile,sep="")

library(MASS,first=T) ## p.517
source(funcfile)

if(length(objects(pattern="dataframe"))>0)
{
        rm(dataframe)
}
if(file.exists(datafile))
{
        import.data(FileName = datafile,
                FileType = "EXCEL",
                ColNames = T,
                DataFrame = "dataframe",
                NameRow = 1);
        rm(datafile)
}
##names(dataframe)[1]<-"x"

u1<-0.000643
u2<-0.000643
s1<-0.006916
s2<-0.016758
p<-0.913174
p0<-c(p,u1,s1,u2,s2)

x.mean<-mean(dataframe[,1])
x.stdev<-sqrt(var(dataframe[,1])) 
sd.min<-x.stdev/25
sd.max<-100*x.stdev
u.min<-x.mean-3*x.stdev
u.max<-x.mean+3*x.stdev
p.min<-0.001
p.max<-0.999

lowerbound<-c(p.min,u.min,sd.min,u.min,sd.min)
upperbound<-c(p.max,u.max,sd.max,u.max,sd.max)

wait.mix2<-nlminb(p0,mix.obj,
        lower=lowerbound,
        upper=upperbound, x=dataframe[,1])

"MIX.OBJ" IS CALLED FROM A LIBRARY; THE SYNTAX IS THAT OF VENABLES AND
RIPLEY, PAGE 292,
EXCEPT THAT I HAVE CHANGED NOTATION:

mix.obj<-function(x,p,u1,s1,u2,s2) {
        e<-(p*dnorm((x-u1)/s1)/s1 +
          (1-p)*dnorm((x-u2)/s2)/s2)
        -sum(log(e))
}
        
