Hi,

Apologies for the delay in summarising.

My question was
> I would like to generate a trellis graph 
> with 2 conditional (given) variables.
> The conditional (factor) variables are
> currency e.g. british pound or australian dollar
> region   e.g. europe or asia/pacific
> 
> My problem is that a currency appears in exactly one region
> but trellis shows (empty) panels for combinations like
> currency == "british pound" & region = "asia/pacific".
> These panels contain no data and there are a lot of them
> so I would like trellis not to waste this space.
> e.g. I would like to see panels only if they contain data
> even though this may mean putting all of the levels from two
> or more conditional variables on the same row.

Example (not included in original posting)

dotplot(formula = Type ~ Price | Reliability * Country,
 data = car.test.frame)

generates a trellis object with lots of empty panels.
Most people recommended something equivalent to 

new.factor <- interaction(car.test.frame$Country ,
 car.test.frame$Reliability , drop=T)
dotplot(formula = Type ~ Price | new.factor,
 data = car.test.frame)

to collapse the number of panels to the subset of panels
which actually contain data.

This is what I want but I was hoping I could retain
the two strips above each panel that are generated 
when conditioning on two variables.
i.e. one strip for Country and one for Reliability
rather than merge both into a single strip.
Noone suggested a solution for this.

I want to retain this feature because I find the separate strips 
(with different colour backgrounds) visually 
more informative than the partial solution given above.

Trellis functionality doesnt seem to allow this
which is a shame as I think I would use it regularily,
given the types of datasets I work with.

