>>>>> "Stefan" == Stefan Steinhaus <stst at informatik.uni-frankfurt.de> writes:

  Stefan> I would like to announce the third edition of my testreport
  Stefan> "Comparison of mathematical programs for data analysis"
  Stefan> which has just been finished. The testreport which is
  Stefan> written in English is available as PDF

  Stefan> file (Acrobat Reader) at the following website :

  Stefan> http://www.informatik.uni-frankfurt.de/~stst/ncrunch/index.html

Thank you for the pointer to your comparison paper.  One thing that I
noticed in the table on speed comparisons (section 7, p. 21) is that
S-PLUS 4.5 is comparatively slow in computing the determinant of a
large, square matrix.  I believe this is because of your method of
calculating the determinant.  According to your script you use
  determinant <- function(x) prod(eigen(x)$values)
This is bound to be slow.  Calculating eigenvalues to get a
determinant is wasteful.  Furthermore, you are also calculating the
eigenvectors even though they are never being used.  Finally,
calculating the eigenvalues requires the use of complex
arithmetic to get a real-valued result.  Even though the eigenvalues
should occur in complex conjugate pairs and the imaginary parts should
cancel this doesn't always work in computer
arithmetic. See below.

You will find the slightly modified function
  det1 <- function(x) prod(eigen(x, only.values = TRUE)$values)
will calculate the determinant faster.  Even better is to use the QR
decomposition instead of the eigenvalue-eigenvector decomposition.
Several years ago, we discussed the calculation of determinants in S
on the S-news list and settled on
  det2 <- function(x) prod(diag(qr(x)$qr)) * (-1)^(ncol(x) - 1)
or something very like it.  In my brief tests (I don't have access to
S-PLUS 4.5 so was testing on another system) det2 was an order of
magnitude faster than your determinant function.

By the way, it is not clear why one would want to calculate a
determinant in the first place.  The value usually came out to be
Infinity in my tests because it was on the order of 5^1000 for a
1000x1000 matrix of uniform (0,1) random numbers.

I include below an example of a 100x100 matrix for which the
determinant is large but finite.

S-news members:  Does anyone know why unix.time does not print the
times for this?  Is it because the expression is itself a print
statement?

Version 3.4 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.3 : 1996 
S> x <- array(runif(10000), c(100,100))
S> determinant <- function(x) prod(eigen(x)$values)
S> unix.time(print(determinant(x)))
[1] 2.2081e+25-1383082274i
S> det1 <- function(x) prod(eigen(x, only.values = TRUE)$values)
S> unix.time(print(det1(x)))
[1] 2.2081e+25-1383082274i
S> det2 <- function(x) prod(diag(qr(x)$qr)) * (-1)^(ncol(x)-1)
S> unix.time(print(det2(x)))
[1] 2.2081e+25

Here is a comparison of the timing on a full 1000x1000 matrix.  I
didn't print the results because it is Infinity in the machine
arithmetic.

Version 3.4 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.3 : 1996 
> options(object.size=10000000)
> xx <- array(runif(1000000), c(1000, 1000))
> det2 <- function(x) prod(diag(qr(x)$qr)) * (-1)^(ncol(x)-1)
> det1 <- function(x) prod(eigen(x, only.values = TRUE)$values)
> determinant <- function(x) prod(eigen(x)$values)
> unix.time(det2(xx))
[1] 53.40  1.22 63.00  0.00  0.00
> unix.time(det1(xx))
[1] 498.0800171   0.5400002 501.0000000   0.0000000   0.0000000
> unix.time(determinant(xx))
Error in .A0[, w] <- .A1: Cannot allocate 16000000 bytes: options("object.size")
        is 10000000: see options help file
Dumped
Timing stopped at: 1166.73 0.82 1174 0 0 
