From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Wed Apr 21 16:32:47 1999
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Wed, 21 Apr 1999 17:32:47 +0100
Subject: Re: [S] Dumb S History Question
In-Reply-To: <4.1.19990421090137.0098c2a0@pop4.ibm.net>
References: <4.1.19990421090137.0098c2a0@pop4.ibm.net>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9904211723510.1141-100000@auk.stats>

On Wed, 21 Apr 1999, Marc R. Feldesman wrote:

> 
> Many of the S (S-Plus) functions have an na.action=na.fail default
> argument.  While using the var() and cor() function, I noted that these
> functions (and probably others) have an na.method="fail" default argument.
> In the first instance, changing the default requires na.action=na.omit (for
> example), while in the second cases, changing the default requires
> na.method="omit".
> 
> Are these differences intentional (e.g. to prevent earlier versions of code
> from braking) or are these simply historical holdovers that have not been
> cleaned up in newer versions of S-Plus?  
> 
> I'm sure there is some interesting (or perhaps boring) history here.  I'd
> be curious why there isn't more consistency in this type of default argument.

I think they are consistent!  All the functions that require an external
function to massage a data frame to deal with NAs have na.action. Those few
functions that fairly recently introduced other ways to deal with NAs (and
for var and cor this is since 3.4) have na.method.  Note though that
na.method=omit might be same as na.omit, but some of the other
possibilities cannot be done by na.action functions. There is no (nor could
there be) na.available.

I am sure the differences are intentional, perhaps to make you pause ot
think that what they do might be different.  That is not to say that there
are not lots of other inconsistencies (see the descriptions of lsfit
and dist for example).

